          "literatureReview": "Semen Analysis is the cornerstone of male fertility evaluation, providing comprehensive assessment of semen quality and sperm function. This test follows WHO guidelines for standardization and uses both manual microscopy and computer-aided sperm analysis (CASA) systems. Macroscopic examination evaluates volume, color, viscosity, liquefaction time, and pH. Microscopic analysis determines sperm concentration, total count, and presence of leukocytes or debris. Sperm characteristics assessment includes motility (progressive, non-progressive, immotile), vitality, and morphology using strict criteria. Normal morphology assessment follows Kruger strict criteria, evaluating head, midpiece, and tail abnormalities. The test requires abstinence period of 2-7 days and specimen collection by masturbation into sterile container. Results interpretation considers multiple parameters together, as individual parameter abnormalities may not indicate infertility. Repeat testing is often recommended due to biological variability."
